# Occurrences of `new SiteNode(`

There are **three** occurrences, all inside `findAndAddChild` and `findAndAddLeaf`.

## 1 & 2 — In `findAndAddChild` (3-argument form)

Both variants use the same constructor signature `(this, historyType, nodeName)`. The distinction is only whether custom icons are set:

```java
if (!baseRef.getCustomIcons().isEmpty()) {
    newNode = new SiteNode(this, baseRef.getHistoryType(), nodeName);
    newNode.setCustomIcons(baseRef.getCustomIcons(), baseRef.getClearIfManual());
} else {
    newNode = new SiteNode(this, baseRef.getHistoryType(), nodeName);
}
```

Here `nodeName` is the method parameter passed straight in (no clean-name transformation for these branch/child nodes).

## 3 — In `findAndAddLeaf` (4-argument form with clean node name)

This is the one that passes both the computed leaf name and the original clean node name:

```java
String leafName = SessionStructure.getLeafName(model, nodeName, msg);
SiteNode node = findChild(parent, leafName);
if (node == null) {
    node = new SiteNode(this, ref.getHistoryType(), leafName, nodeName);
```

### How the arguments are computed
- **`leafName`** (3rd arg): derived via `SessionStructure.getLeafName(model, nodeName, msg)`.
- **`nodeName`** (4th arg, the "clean node name"): passed in directly as the `findAndAddLeaf` method parameter — the raw folder/node name, not the leaf-formatted version.

So the 4-argument constructor receives the display/leaf name **and** the underlying clean name separately. This matches the changelog note: `"2024/01/19 Store clean node name when adding leaf node."`

